WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.120
A Software Bill of Materials, or SBOM, is a structured inventory of the software components that make up an application or software product.

2
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:15.914
It helps organizations understand the ingredients inside software they build, buy, deploy, and maintain.

3
00:00:15.914 --> 00:00:19.536
A common comparison is an ingredient list on packaged food.

4
00:00:19.536 --> 00:00:26.002
The list does not tell you everything about quality or safety, but it gives you visibility into what is present.

5
00:00:26.002 --> 00:00:33.216
An SBOM plays a similar role for software components, dependencies, libraries, and related information.

6
00:00:33.216 --> 00:00:36.070
Modern software is assembled from many parts.

7
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:48.023
A single product may include open-source libraries, commercial packages, framework code, container images, build tools, and transitive dependencies brought in by other dependencies.

8
00:00:48.023 --> 00:00:52.194
Without an inventory, teams may struggle to answer basic questions.

9
00:00:52.194 --> 00:00:53.840
Which components are used?

10
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.367
Which versions are present?

11
00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:57.353
Where did those components come from?

12
00:00:57.353 --> 00:00:59.997
Which products rely on a particular library?

13
00:00:59.997 --> 00:01:02.023
SBOMs support transparency.

14
00:01:02.023 --> 00:01:13.457
They help developers, security teams, procurement teams, compliance teams, customers, and executives discuss software supply chain risk using a shared set of facts.

15
00:01:13.457 --> 00:01:18.356
An SBOM is not a vulnerability scanner and not a complete security assessment.

16
00:01:18.356 --> 00:01:28.553
It is a visibility artifact that becomes valuable when connected to vulnerability management, secure development, procurement, compliance, and risk management processes.

17
00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:32.405
This course provides an educational overview of SBOM concepts.

18
00:01:32.405 --> 00:01:38.322
It is not professional legal, regulatory, compliance, procurement, or software development guidance.

19
00:01:38.322 --> 00:01:43.241
The goal is to help you understand what SBOMs are and why organizations use them.

20
00:01:43.241 --> 00:01:48.279
The most important mindset is that an SBOM is useful only when people can act on it.

21
00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:57.589
A clear inventory helps teams ask better questions, assign ownership, understand exposure, and make faster decisions when software risk changes.
